# Build context for MyVideoGameList.Server/Dockerfile, which is the repository root so that the
# root NuGet.Config applies. An allowlist: everything is excluded, then the two things the image
# needs are let back in, minus their build output. The client, the tests, the docs and .git never
# enter the context, so a local node_modules or bin/ cannot bloat a build or leak into an image.
*
!NuGet.Config
!MyVideoGameList.Server/
MyVideoGameList.Server/bin/
MyVideoGameList.Server/obj/

# A directory negation admits every file under it, tracked or not. The root .gitignore keeps
# appsettings.*.json overrides out of the repository because a developer may hold a
# Production or Staging one with real values, and `dotnet publish` would copy it into the
# image. So every override stays out, and only the committed Development file - container-only
# credentials, by ADR 0005 not a secret - is let back in. The same for the other files that are
# a developer's rather than the application's.
MyVideoGameList.Server/appsettings.*.json
!MyVideoGameList.Server/appsettings.Development.json
MyVideoGameList.Server/Properties/launchSettings.json
MyVideoGameList.Server/**/*.user
MyVideoGameList.Server/**/secrets.json
MyVideoGameList.Server/**/.env
MyVideoGameList.Server/**/.env.*
